WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.240
A buffer overflow happens when code writes more data to a buffer than the buffer can hold, or writes outside the intended buffer area.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:15.780
In C, this is a memory-safety problem because the language gives programs direct access to memory while relying on the developer to preserve boundaries.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.200
Buffer overflows are not only input-validation problems.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:31.980
Input validation helps, but buffer safety also depends on destination capacity, current length, string termination, integer calculations, pointer lifetime, function contracts, compiler behavior, and error handling.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.480
A program can validate that input looks reasonable and still copy it into the wrong-sized buffer.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:45.060
Consequences can include corrupted data, crashes, unreliable behavior, availability failures, and serious security exposure.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:55.120
The practical defensive goal is to prevent out-of-bounds reads and writes before they reach production, not to hope that testing catches every timing, platform, or input variation.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:06.940
Secure C development requires design review, defensive coding, compiler support, static analysis, sanitizer testing, boundary testing, code review, and production-safe diagnostics.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.480
Each layer catches a different kind of mistake.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.820
None of them replaces the need for clear buffer contracts.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.200
This course focuses on prevention.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:24.220
We will discuss how buffer mistakes arise, how to review code for them, how to design safer interfaces, and how to use tooling and tests to find defects earlier.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:32.280
The aim is responsible engineering: code that knows how much space it has, how much data it holds, and what should happen when input does not fit.
